  "slug": "ti-dmd",
  "title": "Texas Instruments Digital Micromirror Device (DMD / DLP)",
  "year": "1987",
  "subtitle": "A chip whose surface is a field of hinged microscopic mirrors that physically tilt thousands of times a second to paint a picture",
  "why_scout_cares": "The museum's only micro-mechanical output device — a single silicon chip that renders information by flipping hundreds of thousands of hinged mirrors between on and off states under electrostatic control. The 'display' is a machine of moving shutters, not an emissive or phosphor surface. Its first product was a printer (DMD2000 airline-ticket printer, 1990) that exposed paper with the same tilting-mirror array.",
  "search_terms": ["Digital Micromirror Device", "DLP", "Larry Hornbeck", "TI deformable mirror", "MEMS display", "DMD2000 printer", "US5061049"],
  "research": {
    "title": "Texas Instruments Digital Micromirror Device (DMD / DLP)",
    "year": "1987",
    "by": "Texas Instruments (Larry J. Hornbeck)",
    "tags": ["HCI", "Output", "Display", "MEMS"],
    "overview": "The Digital Micromirror Device (DMD) is a micro-opto-electromechanical display invented in 1987 by Larry J. Hornbeck at Texas Instruments, building on a deformable-mirror program that began in 1977. The device is a single semiconductor chip whose surface is a rectangular array of several hundred thousand microscopic aluminum mirrors (~16 µm each), every mirror mounted on a yoke by torsion hinges and individually rotatable +/-10-12 degrees through electrostatic attraction from the SRAM cells beneath it.",
    "deepDive": [
      {
        "heading": "Rendering by physical tilt",
        "text": "To make a picture, the chip rapidly flips each mirror thousands of times per second. In the 'on' state a mirror reflects lamp light into the lens to form a bright pixel; in the 'off' state it reflects the light into a heatsink to form a dark one. Gray is produced by binary pulse-width modulation — the mirror is physically reoriented at a duty cycle that determines how much light reaches the screen. Color came from a spinning dichroic filter wheel. The whole image is a field of hinged mechanical shutters moving at microsecond timescales."
      },
      {
        "heading": "A mirror array that printed",
        "text": "The DMD's first commercial product was not a projector but a printer: the DMD2000 airline-ticket printer (1990) used the tilting-mirror array to expose photosensitive paper directly, replacing the laser scanning of a conventional page printer. This illustrates the device's nature as a general-purpose spatial light modulator — the same mirrors that can project a movie can expose a page."
      },
      {
        "heading": "The HCI meaning",
        "text": "DMD collapses the distinction between output and mechanism: the rendering device is literally a micro-machined field of tilting mirrors fabricated on silicon. Unlike every other output in the museum (phosphor, LED, ink, film, oil), DMD paints with mechanical motion — millions of tiny mirrors flapping open and shut. It became the heart of DLP projection, still in use decades later."
      }
    ],
